      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sinking Spring takes its name from a local spring that occasionally sank into the ground, making the water seem to disappear.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_Spring%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Minsi or Wolf tribe of the Lenni Lenape people were the first indigenous inhabitants to live in the Sinking Spring area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_Spring%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sinking Spring is characterized by numerous underground streams that carve through limestone to form natural sinkholes throughout the borough.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinking_Spring%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Heritage Park in Sinking Spring features an 1872 railroad station and a signal building that now serve as a local history museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sinkingspringhistory.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Sinking Spring Area Historical Society formed in 1977 to preserve the history of the borough and the surrounding township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
